  "verdict": {
    "texte": "Earn2Trade is rated 4.6 out of 5 on Trustpilot across nearly 5,000 reviews, and replies to 98% of negative reviews, usually within 48 hours. It also publishes its pass rate, which is still rare in the industry. The Trader Career Path suits traders who would rather grow a small account than buy a large one outright. The trade-off is rules applied without flexibility: going over the allowed number of contracts ends the evaluation immediately, the drawdown becomes trailing on a Live account, and the profit split starts at 50%. Choose it if you are disciplined and patient; avoid it if you want a 90% split from the first dollar or forgiving rules.",
    "pourQui": [
      "Disciplined futures day traders (NQ, ES, CL, GC…) who keep their position size in check.",
      "Traders who want to climb in steps from $25K to $200K rather than pay for a large account up front.",
      "Traders who want the training and the evaluation in one place.",
      "NinjaTrader, Tradovate and TradingView users."
    ],
    "pasPour": [],
    "pointsForts": [],
    "limites": [
      "Monthly subscription. On the Trader Career Path, each renewal includes a free reset.",
      "No swing trading in the evaluation: every position must be closed by 15:50 Chicago time.",
      "30% consistency rule during the evaluation, removed once funded.",
      "Progression ladder: the number of contracts allowed depends on your balance, and going over it ends the evaluation immediately. Ten micro contracts count as one standard contract.",
      "Funded profit split: 50% below a threshold that depends on the account size ($1,500 on TCP25, $2,250 on TCP50, $3,000 on TCP100), 80% above it.",
      "Drawdown: end of day in the evaluation and on LiveSim, trailing on a Live account.",
      "Most funded traders stay on simulated capital: in 2025, 5.23% of successful traders traded a Live account and 94.77% a LiveSim account.",
      "Futures only: no stocks, options, forex, crypto or CFDs.",
      "Citizens of 25 countries cannot be funded, including Nigeria, Pakistan, Kenya, Ukraine and Russia."
    ]
  },
